      <ul style="margin:8px 0 8px 18px;line-height:1.5">
        <li>The difficulty director floors itself near the top and stops easing off when you go quiet.</li>
        <li>Trouble arrives more than twice as often; ich spreads nearly twice as fast and can take four fish.</li>
        <li>Medication costs almost double, raids are more than twice as likely, and the markets swing hard.</li>
        <li>Fish get hungry half again as fast and stress twice as fast.</li>
        <li>Four events that exist nowhere else: cracked glass, a loose predator, ammonia spikes, shoplifters. They all do real damage if you are not watching.</li>
        <li><b>The court.</b> A dynastic layer that does not exist outside Nightmare: the people around the shop, drawn from the same record as everyone else, with pedigrees, faiths and their houses' unsettled scores. You can win them over, learn what they are hiding, squeeze them with it, ruin their trade, have them held on a warrant, marry into their house, or have them killed. Every one of those has a computed chance, a computed chance of being caught, and consequences the rest of the game reads. They can do all of it back.</li>
        <li><b>Consanguinity is modeled, not flagged.</b> Marrying inside your own line reads the actual coefficient of relatedness out of the pedigree, and it is subtracted from your heirs' health and from the ceiling on every skill they can learn. A zealous faith will cut you off for it.</li>
        <li><b style="color:var(--gold)">Hazard pay:</b> every coin a fish drops is worth 50% more, and buyers pay about 18% over.</li>
      </ul>
